FYPONG citizens hold a referendum to Declare whether to change the country's name
FYUM people participated in a referendum on Sunday to decide whether to resuscitate "Democracy of North-Caced Macedonia", a move that would resolve a decades-long disagreement with Greece that had blocked the country's candidacy for accession to the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, argues that the name of the northern neighboring country represents a claim on its territories and has vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
The two governments reached an agreement in June based on the proposed new name, but nationalist adversaries argue that the change will undermine the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority of Macedonia's population.
President George Ivanov said he would not participate in the referendum and a boycott campaign has raised doubts about whether the turnout would reach the required 50 per cent for the referendum to be valid.
"You are in favor of joining NATO and the EU by accepting the agreement with Greece".
Defendants of name-changing, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that it's a price worth paying to seek membership of organisations such as the EU and NATO for FYPD, one of the countries that emerged from the collapse of Yugoslavia.
"I came today to vote for the future of the country, to enable FYROM's young people to live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, because in this way the lives of all of us are going to be safer," Libera Georgievska, said in Skopje.
Even though it's not legally bonding, several members of parliament have indicated that they will comply with the outcome of the vote to make it definitive.
A two-third-third-square-majority majority in parliament is required.
The state election committee said there had been no reports of irregularities by 1 p.m.
Attendance reached just 16 per cent, compared with 34 per cent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 per cent of enrolled-occoupled voters were present.
"I've come to vote for my lads, our place is in Europe," said Joje Tanevski, 62, a voter in the capital, Skopje.
FYPD's premier Johraf Zaev, his wife Zorika and his son Dusko voted in the referendum to change the country's name-changing the way for the country's accession to NATO and the European Union in the Macedonian city of Sturmika on September 30, 2018.
In front of Parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepares a small tent and places chairs in front of tents erected by those who will boycott the referendum.
"We are in favour of NATO and the EU, but we want us to integrate with our heads high, not as second-a-class partners," Kavardarkov said.
"We're a poor country, but we've got dignity.
If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia, and become a member of the Eu-Saline union.”
Prime Minister Zaev said NATO membership would bring much-needed investments to FYU, which has an unemployment rate of more than €20 per cent.
"I believe that the vast majority will be in favor, as more than 80 per cent of our citizens are in favor of EU and NATO," Zaev said after voting.
He said a positive outcome would be "convenience of our future."
According to a poll published last Monday by FYROM's Political Research Institute, between between 30 and 43 per cent of Voters would participate in the referendum.
According to another poll conducted by Macedonia's Telma TV, 57 per cent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 per cent said they would vote positively.
In order for the referendum to be successful, turnout must reach 50 per cent plus one vote.
If the referendum fails, this will be the first serious blow to the pro-Western-governance's policy, from its ascension to power last May.
