The Labour Party is no longer a broad faction.
It's not tolerant to those who express their opinion
I wasn't at all surprised that Momentum movement activists in my local group voted for me to have a censurpie.
After all, I'm the last of a series of Labour MPs to whom they said they weren't welcomed, precisely because we're in our opinion.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan had a similar treatment because she resolutely resist anti-Semitism.
In my case, my no-confidence suggestion critAmpood was criticized because I disagreed with Jeremie Corbyn.
For the importance of a responsible economic policy, for national security, for Europe, paradoxically, for similar issues to what Jeremie had disagreed with previous leaders.
Invitation for Nottingham's East Labour meeting on Paraskevi said that "we want our meetings to be prevalent with the participation of everyone."
For most of the eight-year term I've been a local Labour MP, the meetings of the General Committee on Friday night were just like this.
"Unfortunately, today, it's not the tone of many encounters and the promise of a 'more merciful, more sympathetic' policy has long been forgotten if it's ever really started to materialise.
It's becoming increasingly apparent that differing views don't be tolerated in the Labour Party and that every opinion is judged based on whether it's accepted by party leadership.
This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader, as co-colleagues with whom I previously thought we had similar political views began to wait for me to make a 180-degree U-turn and to adopt views with which I would never agree, whether they concerned national security or the EU single market.
Every time I spoke in public, no matter what I was really saying, I listened to a hexapsalm on social media, where some people were calling for my rejection, renounced the center's politics and told me that I shouldn't be a member of the Labour Party.
And that didn't just happen to me.
"In fact, I know I'm luckiest than my other colleagues, as the comments aimed at me were usually apolitical.
Miracling the professionalism and resolvity of my colleagues facing a torrent of sexist or racist inspiracies every day but never receding.
One of the most frustrating aspects of this era in politics is how infestations are now considered normal.
Jeremie Corbyn claimed last week that Labour should encourage a culture of tolerability.
The reality is that we are no longer a wide-ranging faction and that with every no-command's no-command or a change in the rules of choice, the limits of the party are narrowing.
I've been adjudicated many times over the last couple of years not to stand up, don't talk too much and that "everything is going to be okay".
But I didn't go into politics to do this.
Since Joining the Labour Party, 32 years ago, as a pupil still, due to the disparcipitiship shown by the Thatcher government which literally led to the failure of my entire class, my purpose was to defend better public services for those who need them more, either as a member of the local council or as a government minister.
I've never hid my politics, and I've done the same in the last election.
No one in Nottingham Ish could be confused in any way with regard to my political views and points of dissent with today's leadership.
To those who promoted the no-confidence motion on Friday, all I have to say is that, while the country is confronted with a blunt of households, businesses and our public service, I don''t understand the desire to waste time and energy on whether I'm loyal to the leader of the Labour Party.
But, in fact, my only message isn't appealed to members of the Momentum movement in Nottingham, but to my constituents, whether they're members of the Labour Party or not: I'm proud to serve you and I promise that no threat of rejection or political expediency won't prevent me from acting as I believe it's in the best interests of all of you.
Kris Leslie is an Incipient of Nottingham East
