"The sea is ours": Enclosed Bolivia hopes the court will relaunch the road to the Pacific
Sailors roam the fully-equipped navy headquarters in La Paz.
In the public buildings they wave blue flags.
In the bases of the navy from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon, the motto is written: "The sea rightfully belongs to us.
It's our duty to take it back."
Across land-clogged Bolivia, the remembrance of Chile's coastline after a bloody clash over the exploitation of natural resources in the 19th century is still still alive, as is the craving of its people to travel to the Pacified Ocean once again.
These hopes have regenerated today more than ever, for decades as Bolivia awaits the decision of the International Court of Justice on Oct. 1, after five years of deliberations.
"Bolivia has the momentum, a spirit of unity and tranquility, and of course awaits the result with a positive look," said Roberto Calzadillah, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will witness the issuance of the International Tribunal's decision on large screens across the country, hoping the Hague court will decide in favor of Bolivia's claim to oblivion to oblige Chile, after decades of unsettled talks, to negotiate the granting to Bolivia of a sovereign way out to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic Indigenous President, who is fighting a controversial battle for his re-election next year, has a lot to win or lose from Monday's decision.
"We're very close to going back to the Pacific Ocean," he promised at the end of August.
However, some analysts believe that the court is not likely to decide for Bolivia and that, even if it did, they wouldn't change much.
The U.N. institution in the Netherlands does not have the power to give Chilean territory and has made it clear that it will not determine the outcome of possible discussions.
The fact that the International Tribunal's decision is to be extradillowed just six months after final arguments were presented suggests that the case "wasn't complicated," Paz Zarate, a Chilean expert on international law.
And rather than further advancing Bolivia's goals, the past four years may have undermined them.
"Bolivia's current government has usurped the issue of access to the sea," Zarate said.
Morales's aggressive rhetoric has exhausted every responsive good-sponsored dissatisfactory of Chile, he argued.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to speak at some point, but it will be extremely difficult to have discussions after that.
The two countries have not swapped ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodriguez Velje, Bolivian representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision-making process was unusually expatriate.
Bolivia on Monday will gain "an extraordinary opportunity to inaugurate a new era of relations with Chile" and the ability to "put an end to disagreements of 139 years with reciprocal gains," he said.
Kalzadila also denied that Morales, who is still one of Latin America's most popular leaders, uses the issue of exiting the sea as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never renounce its right to access the Pacific Ocean," he added.
"The decision is an opportunity to see that we have to overcome the past."
