John Edoward: Linguistic skills are essential for the citizens of the world
The independent schools of Scotland have prehistory in academic excellence, which continued in 2018, with yet a series of outstanding exam results, which were simply further enhanced by individual and collegiate successes in sports, the arts, music and other communicipal efforts.
With more than 30,000 pupils throughout Scotland, these schools, represented by Scotland's Independent School Council (SCIS), are struggling to provide the best possible level of service to pupils and their parents.
The aim of the independent schools is to prepare their pupils for the next and higher educational levels, the professional career they will choose and their place as citizens of the world.
As an educational field that can design and implement a on-demand school curriculum, we see that modern languages continue to be a popular and desirable elective course in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you're talking to someone in a language he understands, then what you're saying gets into his head.
"If you speak to him in his own language, what you're saying goes into his heart."
This is a strong reminder that we can’t just rely on English if we want to build relationships and reliance with people from other countries.
Based on the recent results of this year's exams, we find that languages are at the forefront of performance tables, with the highest rates of success in independent schools.
A total of 68 per cent pupils who studied foreign languages achieved baccalaureate A.
Evidence gathered from 74 SCIS schools showed that 72 per cent of pupil pupils achieved baccalaureate A in mandarins, while 72 per cent of those who educated german, 69 per cent those who educated French and 63 per cent of those who educated Spanish also had an A stanit.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as vital skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
The languages today, as an elective course, are considered equally important as courses related to science, technology, engineering and mathematics in the curriculum of independent schools and elsewhere.
A survey by the U.B. Employment and Skills Committee in 2014 revealed that, as far as employers found it difficult to fill vacances, 17 per per cent involved a lack of language skills.
Therefore, the need to acquire language skills is becoming more and more urgent, so that young people are prepared for their future professional careers.
Given that more future employment opportunities require linguistic knowledge, these skills are essential in a globalised world.
Irrelevant the career one chooses, if you have learned a second language, you will have a real advantage in the future, because of this skill you will be able to utilize throughout your life.
If someone is able to communicate directly with people from foreign countries, then automatically this multi-language person will have an advantage over the competition.
According to YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the UK in 2013, 75 per cent of respondents weren't able to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, while French was the only language spoken double-digit percentage of respondents (15 per cent).
For this reason, cladding in language teaching today is important for today's children.
The knowledge of many languages, in particular the languages of the developing economies, will provide children with better opportunities to find a meaningful job.
In Scotland, every school will teach different languages.
Some schools will concentrate on the most classical modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered the most important for the U.A.-threatening 2020, such as mandarin or Japanese.
For no matter what your child is interested, there will always be a number of languages to choose in the independent schools, which have qualified teaching staff in this area.
Concreither Scottish schools give a learning environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills they need to achieve, no matter what the future holds.
It cannot be denied today that, in a global business environment, languages continue to be extremely important for the future of the country, so this reality should be reflected in education.
In fact, modern-day languages should actually be considered "international communicative skills."
Widesp-independent schools will continue to confer this choice, multi-culturalism and excellence for Scotland's young people.
They've got to do it.
John Edoward is the director of Scottish Intelligent Schools.
