Harbird students attend classes on how to rest enough
This year, a new course at the Harvard University enlisted all undergraduate students to sleep more in an effort to combat the growing macho culture of all-encrevary studies by consuming a lot of caffeine.
An academic has discovered that students at number 1 university worldwide often don't know basic things, e.g. how to take care of themselves.
Charles Chaisler, Professor of Sleep Medicine at the Harvard Medical School and a qualified doctor at Brigham and Women's Hospital, designed the course, which he believes to be the first of its kind in the United States.
Inspired to start the course after a speech about the effects of sleep deprivation on learning.
"In the end, a girl came to me and said, 'Why am I learning about it just now, in my last year?'
She said that no one had ever talked to her about the importance of sleep - which surprised me," she told The Telegraph.
The course, started for the first time this year, explains to students the basic principles of how good sleeping habits contribute to academic and athletic performance, as well as improving general well-encompromises.
Paul Barreira, professor of Psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university’s sanitary services, said the university decided to include the course when it was found that students were deprivation of sleep within the week to a considerable extent.
Hourly course includes a series of interactive assignments.
In a section there is the image of a student dormitory, where students cling to cups of coffee, curtains, sneakers and books, to be informed about the effects of caffeine and light, how athletic performance is influenced by lack of sleep, as well as the importance of sleep routine.
In another module, participants learn how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of infarction, cerebral, depression and cancer.
A map of the campus, with interactive icons, encourages attendees to think about their daily routine.
"We know it won't change the behavior of the students immediately.
We believe, however, that they have the right to know, just as you have the right to be aware of the effects on health when you choose to smoke.”
The culture of beprouting because you studied overnight still exists, he said, adding that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure on students means sleep deprivation is inflated.
Enough sleep, of good quality, should be the "secret weapon" of students to combat stress, depletion and anxiety, he said, even to avoid weight gain, as sleep deprivation puts the brain in a state of starvation, resulting in a constant feeling of starvation.
Raymond Shaw, a 19-year-old Callifornevian studying Chemistry and Natural Biology, has helped Professor Chaisler design the course, having attended one of his registries last year, his first year at Harvard.
He said his lesson opened his eyes and inspired him to push for a course across the campus.
The next step, he hopes, is to ask all postgraduate students to attend a similar program before starting their studies in this highly competitive educational institution.
Professor Chaisler advised students to set up a notification of the time they should go to bed, as well as an alarm clock for waking, and to be aware of the harmful effects of "blue light" emancipated from electronic screens and LED lighting, which can detune the circadian rhythm, leading to sleep problems.
