HIV/Aids: China Reports 14% Rise in New Incidents
China announced a 14% increase in the number of its citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
More than 820,000 people are affected in the country, according to health services.
In the second quarter of 2018, around 40,000 new cases were reported.
In the vast majority, the new cases were transmitted through sexual intercourse, signaling a change compared to the past.
Traditionally, the HIV virus spread rapidly in some parts of China due to transfusions of infected blood.
However, the number of people infected with HIV in this way was almost nihilised, China's health officials had stated at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, compared to the previous year, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has risen by $100,000.
Transmitting HIV through sexual intertwining is a very serious issue for China's LOAT community.
Homophilia decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against the LGBT community remains a common occurrence.
Due to the country's predatory values, studies estimate that 70-90% of men who have sexual intercourse with men will eventually marry women.
Numerous incidents of transmission of these diseases are due to insufficient sexual protection in these relationships.
Since in 2003, China's government has pledged universal access to anti-HIV drugs as part of an effort to tackle the problem.
