The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to launch a new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is embarking a start-up aimed at competing with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The new project of this technology legend is Inrupt, a company that registrapples based on Berners Lee's open Solids platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data will be stored, as well as who will be able to access and what information.
In an out-of-the-scenes interview with the Fast Company, Berners Lee joiled the intent behind Inrupt is "global dominance."
"We've got to do it right now," he said of the startup.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid platform technology by enabling users to create their own "personal electronic data storage" or a POD.
It may contain contact lists, pending task lists, diary, music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all available in a browser and all of them at the same time.
The unique feature of the personal electronic data storage is that it allows the user to decide who has access to what kind of information.
The company csoreses this possibility "personal empowerment through the data".
Inrupt's idea, according to the company's CEO, John Bruce, is for the company to offer resources, processes, procedures, and appropriate competencies for Solid platform to be available to everyone.
The company is made up of Berners Lee, Bruce, a security platform bought by IBM, some contracted developers who worked on the project and a community of volunteer code developers.
From this week, tech developers around the world can create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners Lee said he and his team aren't talking "Facebook and Google about whether they're being allowed to make radical changes, thwarting business models overnight.
"We don't ask them for permission."
In a post on Medium released on Sabbath, Berners Lee wrote that "Inrupt's mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that will protect the integrity and quality of the new web created based on the Solid platform."
In 1994, Berners Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at Masahushestert Technological (MIT).
For the past few months, Berners Lee has been a strong voice in the debate about the neutrality of the Internet.
Even with Inrupt, Berners Lee will remain the founder and director of World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and Open Data Institute.
"I'm extremely upbeat for the next epoch of the Internet," Berners Lee added.
