The National Confidence Encipates the Mystical Life of Bats
New research being carried out on an estate in Scottish Highlands seeks to unravel how ghettons are using the landscape when chasing their freckles.
The findings will shed a new light on the behavior of these solitary flying mammals and will contribute to the design of future actions for the protection of the environment.
The study by scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will investigate the habits of the common nanon-bail and micronut, as well as Dobynton's brown odil and myotida myitrus in Inverewe Gardens gardens, in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be mounted in key locations around the estate to monitor bat activities throughout the season.
NTS staff and volunteers will also carry out movable searches using hand detectors.
Analysing the sound of all recordings by specialists will verify the frequency of bat calls, as well as which species have this behavior.
Subsequently, we will create a map of the biotope and an exhibition, in order to form a detailed picture of their behavior on a landscape scale.
Rob Duyar, a natural environment conservation consultant for NTS, hopes the results will unsubstantiate which areas of the biotope are most important to bats and how they're used by each species.
This information will help identify the benefits of habitat management, such as the creation of meadows and the best way to preserve forests for bats and other related species.
Nomb populations in Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom have declined sharply since the last century.
Threagued by the construction work and development projects that affect the places they roost and destroy their livelihoods.
Wind Turbines and lighting are also a danger, as well as adhesive tapes for flies and some chemical treatments of building materials, as well as attacks by domestic cats.
Natties aren't actually blind.
However, because they chasing at night, their ears are more useful than their eyes to catch their prey.
They're using a sophisticated echolocation technique to detect insects and obstacles in the course of their flight.
NTS, which is responsible for carving more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, considers bats very important.
It has ten trained experts, who regularly conduct enquiries, indulge the places where bat roosters roost and sometimes rescue them.
The organization has also set up Scotland's first and only shelter exclusively for bolts in the Threave estate, Dumfris and Galloway, where eight of Scotland's ten bat species live.
The estate manager, David Thompson, declares that the estate is the ideal ground for bats.
"Here in Threave we have an excellent area for the bats," he said.
"We've got the old buildings, a lot of large trees and an ideal habitat.
There's still a lot we don't know about bats, so the work we're doing here and elsewhere will help us to better understand what they need to increase their populations."
He emphasizes that it is important to first check the existence of bats prior to each maintenance work in dwellings, as the devastation of a single nest with small ones could kill up to 400 females and small, potentially eliminating the entire local population.
Bats are protected species and it is forbidden to kill them, harass them or disturb their quietness, or to destroy their nests.
Elicabeth Ferrel, an executive of the Bat Conservation Trust in Scotland encouraged the public to offer their help.
“There’s still a lot to be learned about our bats, and for many of our species we just don’’t know the evolution of their population.”
